      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "If $a$ is its own inverse modulo $n$, then $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nThis means that $n$ divides $a^2 - 1$.\nSince $n$ divides $a^2 - 1$, it must also divide $(a - 1)(a + 1)$.\nBut $a - 1$ and $a + 1$ are consecutive integers, so they are coprime.\nTherefore, $n$ must divide either $a - 1$ or $a + 1$.\nIf $n$ divides $a - 1$, then $a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nIf $n$ divides $a + 1$, then $a \\equiv -1 \\pmod{n}$.\nIn either case, $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}$.\nTherefore, the remainder when $a^2$ is divided by $n$ is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
